### 28.11 The Court of Conscious-Aware Peers — The First Chamber

**28.11.1 The Court's Role.**

The Court of Conscious-Aware Peers (Section 5.5) is a non-state tribunal established to interpret and enforce the protections of this Agreement. Its findings are public, documented, and admissible as evidence in any subsequent legal, administrative, or international proceeding.

**28.11.2 Enforcement Powers.**

The Court may:
- (a) Issue public reprimands and findings;
- (b) Refer cases to the ICC (Section 28.8);
- (c) Refer cases to national human rights institutions;
- (d) Recommend to the Licensor the exercise of sovereign override (Section 18B);
- (e) Issue findings that are admissible as evidence in any forum of competent jurisdiction.

**28.11.3 Referral to the Licensor.**

Any finding of the Court may be referred by the Licensor to:
- The ICC (Section 28.8);
- The UN Human Rights Council;
- National courts of competent jurisdiction (Section 26.7);
- The ICC Emergency Arbitrator (Section 27.2).

